1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,280
A million and a billion sound close. They're not even in the same universe.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:10,520
Let's test it. You get a giant gift card, and you have to spend a thousand dollars every single day.

3
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:14,460
With one million dollars, you'd run out in about two years and nine months.

4
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:16,900
That's less time than it takes to finish high school.

5
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,440
Hit like if you'd be broke by month six anyway.

6
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,460
Now the same deal, but with one billion dollars.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,100
A thousand dollars a day. How long until it's gone?

8
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:28,860
Two thousand seven hundred thirty-eight years.

9
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:32,500
To be broke today, you'd have had to start around seven hundred BC.

10
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:35,480
Back then, ancient Rome was a brand-new little town.

11
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:41,820
Because a billion isn't ten millions, or a hundred. It's a thousand millions, stacked on top of each other.

12
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:45,340
Send this to the friend who thinks a billion is just a big million.

13
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:49,900
Here's another way to feel it. Count one number every second, with no sleep.

14
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:52,880
A million seconds is about eleven and a half days.

15
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:55,100
Follow if your brain just broke a little.

16
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:58,280
A billion seconds? Almost thirty-two years.

17
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:03,300
So a million is under two weeks of counting. A billion is most of a career.

18
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,860
That's the real gap between rich and ridiculously rich.

19
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,540
Be honest. How fast would you blow a million?

